## Fixture vocabulary and fixed observations

These symbols describe durable facts, not proposed schema fields. Use fictional owned patient P, second patient Q, authenticated owning clinician O, foreign clinician F, and independently trusted source S. H1 is one independently established hold episode with exact accepted archived trigger T1 and bundle B1 plus their valid dependencies. It contains no admission, fever or fall; repeat the core entry case using clinician-concern and already accepted threshold-rule evidence separately, without selecting a new threshold.

Before each isolated case, capture D0: P's patient/state/consent/eligibility versions V0=(p0,s0,c0,e0), current decision d0, permission to approve an eligible ordinary action, no H1 latch, no H1 review item, hold-operation receipt/cursor position C0, and exact bytes/hashes of a previously saved history display and its raw source. A previously saved approval, where used, remains an immutable historical fact. Q is a negative control.

Define DH1 as these durable postconditions together: actual accepted live receipt R tied to the exact accepted trigger/bundle and service P/owner; active H1 safety latch; P's patient/state/eligibility versions advanced beyond V0 with a new current decision; current approval/draw readiness disabled and support paused; old approvals/drafts cannot authorize a new action; pending review owned by the service-authorized responsible owner; successful audit/idempotency receipt and cursor consistent with this committed operation. Consent authority is not granted by this operation. Historical display/raw bytes and Q are unchanged. Exact version deltas, cursor encoding and task cardinality beyond one effective obligation require the executable contract below; the oracle demands their atomic consistency, not guessed column names.

DN means no committed hold transition, readiness/version change, successful receipt or success cursor advance caused by the rejected/rolled-back request; D0 clinical facts survive. A documented rejection/pending audit may persist separately if the accepted contract permits it, but must not represent clinical acceptance. For an already held prestate, DN preserves that held prestate instead.

Use finite fixed instants E=2026-10-20T09:00:00Z (effective trigger), A=2026-10-20T09:05:00Z (archive availability), R=2026-10-21T10:00:00Z (actual live receipt), and Rminus/Rplus one microsecond before/after R. These are test clocks, not clinical timing rules. Make dependency recording/availability earlier than the cutoff unless the case explicitly changes them. Do not copy an uploader's historical A into the actual service receipt R. Preserve archived original bytes and clocks.

