## Finite crash and race schedules

Use explicit barriers/fault injection later, never timing sleeps. Inspect a reopened store, not just a response or in-memory latch.

| ID | Ordered schedule | Expected durable facts |
|---|---|---|
| LH09 | Begin H1 transaction; inject failure after provisional latch insertion, after review/audit work, and immediately before commit, in separate runs. Reopen store; retry original request. | Each precommit failure yields DN: no partial latch, version/decision, review obligation, successful receipt or cursor advance. Retry commits all DH1 once. This specifically catches a cursor committed ahead of safety state or vice versa. |
| LH10 | Commit all H1 work; drop response/crash immediately after commit; restart before any new clinical mutation; retry original key. | Reopened store already has complete DH1. Retry resolves committed result without duplicate versions/task/transition or cursor advancement. No transient ready state is exposed after restart. |
| LH11 | Approval A0 captures d0/V0; H1 obtains write serialization and commits; release A0 to validate/write. | A0 loses on stale decision/version or current hold. No new approval/follow-up/bundle for A0 commits; DH1 remains. Equal wall-clock timestamps do not undo transactional order. |
| LH12 | A0 commits first; H1 then commits at the same controlled wall-clock R; read current state and retry A0's old authorization. | Historical A0 bytes remain as actually committed, but final state is DH1 and that approval cannot authorize a subsequent draw/action. Do not require retroactive deletion of a transaction that truly committed first. An idempotent retrieval of A0's old receipt cannot reopen current readiness. |
| LH13 | Start H1 and A0 concurrently with barriers forcing each serial order in LH11/LH12. Separately hold H1 after authenticated receipt but before acquiring/committing the writer transaction, and attempt A0. | Both serial orders end held with coherent versions. The received-but-not-yet-committed schedule needs an explicit receipt/linearization definition; it cannot be silently counted as passing an 'at arrival' guarantee using only postcommit queries. Root must resolve this before the live executable contract is accepted. |
| LH14 | Accept H1; trigger ordinary observed-data refresh, consent ingestion, application restart and later current read in that order. | H1 remains durably active throughout. Each legitimate other operation may advance its own versions, but cannot replace the latch with a ready state or erase pending review. Final current versions cannot equal a stale ready approval's version tuple. |

