## History and temporal visibility cases

LH21: Save immutable display/raw bytes before H1. Perform H1 entry, later trigger correction/retraction, rejected imported resolution, and (only if independently supported) authenticated clearance. Fetch the same historical decision and approval display after each operation and restart. Expected: exact original bytes/hashes and original versions remain unchanged; new audits/current decisions record later knowledge separately. Historical/retrospective context cannot submit a current approval or clearance. An earlier approved plan may still be displayed historically while its current action authority is invalidated.

LH22: For a trigger actually made available to the service at R with event/recorded clocks earlier than R, query strict learner at Rminus, R and Rplus. Expected inclusion of that newly received evidence: absent, absent, present respectively; current safety at R is already held after accepted receipt. If recorded_at also equals R, strict equality still excludes. Vary recorded_at to later than a chosen K in a separate valid projection fixture: it remains excluded even if its available clock is earlier. Apply event_at <= K as well. Filter cutoff before selecting lineage heads. Archive reconstruction uses the archive's own visibility clocks and must not be conflated with the later live receipt: if the same object was already qualified at A, an archive query at K between A and R may reconstruct the hold while the live system has not yet received it.

LH23: Original trigger/bundle visible before K0; correction/retraction becomes visible at L>K0, with successors still referencing the old exact hash. Query historical archive reconstruction at K0, L and Lplus. Expected: K0 unchanged; strict equality at L still uses the previously visible valid chain; Lplus marks affected dependencies stale/unresolved until explicit valid revisions reconnect them. Current latch remains held throughout regardless of that retrospective reconstruction. Tomorrow's review cannot justify yesterday's resolution or enter yesterday's causal predictor context. Saved historical display remains byte-identical.

## Explicit contract questions for root (not new policy)

1. The accepted base has no evidence-gated clearance endpoint. The word 'existing' in R1 is not evidence of one. Define/critique the bounded executable workflow or keep clearance blocked; engineering invalidation can still proceed.
2. Identify the trusted live source-authority binding and permitted actor/owner mapping. Exact accepted archive hashes alone authenticate identity, not authorization. No uploader-chosen route or owner should resolve this omission.
3. Specify idempotency namespace across sessions/reauthentication, handling of distinct-key duplicates, cursor meaning (accepted operations versus source sequence) and whether rejected/pending receipts consume a separate position. Fix exact version deltas/consent-version behavior before tests assert numbers. The above uses monotonic material invalidation and atomicity only.
4. Define the actual authenticated receipt/transaction linearization point and received-but-blocked approval race. Inclusive wall-clock equality alone does not settle this concurrency schedule.
5. Specify out-of-order pending/rejection behavior and future-dated source declaration handling. Neither may falsely publish successful current readiness/clearance or silently omit required dependencies.
6. Any added shared route fields need the separate bounded critique required by R1. These expected cases introduce no schema fields and do not waive the archive guard.

